     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "When the thing acted on is a person introduced with the personal a (*se masacró A miles de soldados*), the verb stays singular even though the \"object\" is plural — this is what separates a true se-passive (agreeing with its subject: *se recolectó ese rescate*) from this impersonal-flavored one. Both are extremely common in historical Spanish; don't worry too much about the grammatical label, just recognize the pattern."
